  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Conditions will continue to improve as\nNor'easter lifts away from the area. Large surf continues as \nwell as coastal flooding impacts along vulnerable locations of \nthe coast, especially along Hatteras Island where dune structure\nis fragile. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe, and ferry \nservice remains suspended in certain locations. Each subsequent \nhigh tide cycle will continue to threaten the dunes further, and\nthe threat of additional impacts to Hwy 12 remain. The next \nhigh tide is around 430 PM this afternoon and 5 AM Thursday \nmorning. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in effect for Hatteras\nIsland and a Coastal Flood Advisory for the Northern Outer \nBanks and Ocracoke Island. Additionally, a high surf advisory \ncontinues for the beaches from Duck to Cape Hatteras where surf \nheights 8-12 ft will continue into Thu. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us\nthrough the next several days with temps a bit below climo. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s range. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the 40s inland to the 50s coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong high pressure building in from the north\nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period\nof breezy to strong NE winds Friday into the weekend, mainly\nalong the coast. This combined with high astronomical tides\ncould lead to additional minor coastal impacts (coastal flooding\nand rough surf).",
